P9 (case) for the platform lane, after P7b landed (3673660): P7b's rows for P9's pods (61f782d) held to the pods as
built (ADR-148 Amendment 7, "P7b's rows checked"; `tests/cloud_platform_backup/test_backups_rows.py`, which reads what
each container's command calls from `services.api.platform_backup`'s own call graph). Apply with `git apply` from the
repository root, after P9 merges. Three differences:

1. `restore-claims` runs the writers check (its `verify` init container and its own command both call
   `writers_stopped`) and had no `kube-api`, so on EKS every claims restore would be refused, "cannot confirm the
   writers are stopped". profile.yaml: `restore-claims: [kube-api]`.
2. `restore` had ArcadeDB, which none of its commands calls: ArcadeDB's restore (`restore-graph`) is `restore.sh`
   from the zip on the Job's own claim. A path never used, as KMS was (the orchestrator's ruling of 11 November).
   profile.yaml: `restore: [kube-api, clickhouse]`.
3. cp-policy-check's default-deny on our Secret's keys listed none for the backup, which needs ArcadeDB's password
   (ARCADEDB_ROOT_PASSWORD) to ask ArcadeDB for its backup: every render with the backups was refused
   (`whole-secret: CronJob/ospreyflow-backup/backup`). P9 now gives that key to the backup's own container alone
   (committed on the branch); `NAMED_SECRET_ALLOWED["backup"]` lists it here. The other three pods hold no key of ours.

Also here: P7b's two tests and Amendment 5 point 21's words follow the rows; three plants in `p7b_reds.py`, each red
with this diff applied (the record: `gates/p9_rows.r1.log`); and P9's own two test files lose their waiting
conditions (`AWAITED`, the strict xfail on the restores' rows), so once applied nothing reads its own expectation
from the rows it checks. With this diff on the branch: the four suites 583 passed, none held; without it, the
branch's 579 passed and the two restores' rows xfailed (strict).

